<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Tanganyika</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Tanganyika is a long, deep lake in the African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a>, bordered by present-day Tanzania, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, Burundi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumwimbe-ngombe">Kumwimbe Ngombe</a>, military expeditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba Empire</a> extended eastward towards the lake. The region connected Luba to wider trade networks reaching the East African coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika.jpg" alt="Lake Tanganyika" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:STS51G-034-0012_Lake_Tanganyika_June1985.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Tanganyika is an African Great Lake and the world's longest freshwater lake. It is the second largest freshwater lake by volume and the second deepest, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a>. It extends 676 km north to south, covers 32,000 square kilometres and has a mean depth of 572 m and a maximum depth of 1,471 m. It lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a> and is shared among Tanzania, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, Burundi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. It is one of only twenty lakes more than a million years old.</p><p>The one major outflow, the Lukuga River, runs into the Congo system, and in the past the lake has rarely had an outflow to the sea. Its deeper waters hold no oxygen, which limits fish to the upper layers. It has at least 250 endemic species of cichlid fishes and supports a major fishery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the mid-19th century Swahili Arab traders from Zanzibar established a caravan route from Bagamoyo through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a> on the lake. They brought sailing dhows and traded in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, guns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a> established a state west of the lake. The British explorers Richard Burton and John Speke, searching for the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, were the first known Westerners to reach it, in 1858. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> visited it several times in 1866-73 and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> at Ujiji in 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and King Leopold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> established colonies on the lake in the late 19th century and in the early 20th ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Germans, with the ship Graf Goetzen, held complete control of the lake early on. Lieutenant Commander Geoffrey Spicer-Simson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> brought two armed motor boats, HMS Mimi and HMS Toutou, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by rail, road and river to Albertville, now Kalemie. In December 1915 they captured the gunboat Kingani, and the Hedwig was sunk in February 1916. The Goetzen was scuttled on 26 July 1916, raised in 1924 and renamed MV Liemba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Tanganyika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A smaller campaign on the southern shore of <b>Lake Tanganyika</b> in 1914 and 1915 had pitted a makeshift British and Belgian flotilla against the garrison at Bismarckburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Burundi took shape in the late seventeenth century in the high, well-watered hills east of <b>Lake Tanganyika</b>, along the ridge that divides the Congo and Nile basins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the empire's height under Ilunga Sungu (c. 1780–1810), Kumwimbe Ngombe (c. 1810–1840) and Ilunga Kabale (c. 1840–1870), Luba expeditions pushed east towards <b>Lake Tanganyika</b>, north into Manyema and south towards the Kazembe kingdom of the Luapula, consolidating tributary networks and securing trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Caravans organised by Arab, Swahili and Nyamwezi traders and financed by Indian merchants travelled inland to Tabora, Ujiji on <b>Lake Tanganyika</b> and beyond, returning with ivory and captives; Tippu Tip, the most famous of these traders, built a personal domain in the eastern Congo basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Caravans organised by Swahili and Arab merchants, such as the notorious trader Tippu Tip, travelled hundreds of kilometres inland to <b>Lake Tanganyika</b>, Buganda and the eastern Congo, and Swahili spread along their routes as a language of trade.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The journalist Henry Morton Stanley located the missionary-explorer David Livingstone at Ujiji, on <b>Lake Tanganyika</b>, by Stanley's own dating.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
